{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-latin/index.html","title":"Overview and preface","text":"Open TextbooksLatinA course in the Latin languageWikibooks contributors <p>Open Textbooks</p> Latin <p>A course in the Latin language</p> <p>Wikibooks contributors</p> <p>Lessons in Latin from the first declensions to the reading of Caesar, Cicero and Virgil, with grammar, vocabulary and exercises.</p> Start reading <p>46 chapters13 appendices</p>"},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-latin/index.html#contents","title":"Contents","text":"<p>Introduction to the origins and structure of Latin</p> <ul> <li>How to study a language on the Internet and in your head</li> <li>Special consideration: How to use a Wikibook when progress may mean inaccuracy</li> <li>Grammatical Introduction to Latin</li> <li>Basic Grammar</li> </ul> <p>Chapter 1: Basic Sentences</p> <ul> <li>The Nominative Case</li> <li>Adjectives</li> <li>Present indicative active construct</li> <li>Adverbs &amp; Prepositions</li> <li>The Accusative Case</li> <li>Pronouns</li> <li>Chapter 1 Verse</li> </ul> <p>Chapter 2: Complex Sentences</p> <ul> <li>The Imperfect Tense</li> <li>The Genitive and Dative Cases</li> <li>The Future Tense</li> <li>The Ablative and Vocative Case</li> <li>The 3rd, 4th and 5th declensions</li> <li>Irregular Verbs &amp; Revision</li> <li>Translation Exercise</li> <li>Imperfect and Future indicative active constructs</li> <li>Chapter 2 Verse</li> </ul> <p>Chapter 3: Advanced Sentences</p> <ul> <li>Imperatives</li> <li>Active vs. Passive Verbs</li> <li>Indicative Passive Verbs</li> <li>Principal Parts</li> <li>The Perfect Indicative Tense</li> <li>The Perfect Indicative Passive Verbs</li> <li>Future and Past Perfect Indicative Tenses</li> <li>Ablative Absolute and Accusative Infinitive Constructions</li> <li>Chapter 3 Verse</li> </ul> <p>Chapter 4: The Subjunctive Mood and Complex Sentences</p> <ul> <li>The Subjunctive Mood</li> <li>The Uses of the Subjunctive</li> <li>The Subjunctive Imperfect</li> <li>The Subjunctive Passive Verbs</li> <li>The Subjunctive Perfects</li> <li>The Subjunctive Perfect Passive Verbs</li> <li>The Gerund and Participles</li> <li>Conditional Clauses</li> <li>Revision</li> <li>Idioms</li> <li>Translation</li> <li>Chapter 4 Verse</li> <li>Verse from the Gospels</li> </ul> <p>Chapter 5: Review</p> <ul> <li>Translation</li> <li>Poem about Latin</li> </ul> <p>Chapters</p> <ul> <li>Spoken Latin Wikibook</li> </ul> <p>Appendices</p> <ul> <li>Appendix A: Verb Synopsis</li> <li>Appendix B: List of Declensions</li> <li>Appendix C: Phonology of Latin</li> <li>Appendix D: Accents and Scansion</li> <li>Appendix E: Latin Grammatical Definitions</li> <li>Appendix F: Latin Dictionary</li> <li>Appendix G: Latin Mottos and Phrases</li> <li>Appendix H: Latin Abbreviations</li> <li>Appendix I: Common phrases</li> <li>Appendix J: Latin Resources</li> <li>Appendix K: Spoken Latin</li> <li>Appendix L: Words and their Flexion</li> <li>Appendix M: Stylistic Features of Latin Verse and Prose</li> </ul> <p>Chapters</p> <ul> <li>Authors</li> </ul>"},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-latin/index.html#about-this-book","title":"About this book","text":"<p>A course in Latin for English speakers: pronunciation, nouns and their cases, verbs and their tenses, syntax and the reading of original Latin prose and verse, with vocabulary lists and exercises for each lesson.</p> <p>From Wikibooks · CC BY-SA 4.0</p><p>Latin, a Wikibooks textbook, by its contributors. This book is not stored in this library: each chapter is read from Wikibooks when it is opened, shown in this library's format, and ends with its own attribution. Licence: CC BY-SA 4.0. Modified: converted to this format.</p>"},{"location":"open-textbooks/wb-latin/accents-and-scansion.html","title":"Appendix D: Accents and Scansion","text":""},{"locatio